doc.para:
Properties
　Properties can be treated in the same way as variables, but in reality, like method calls, they involve calling a setter and a getter. Setters and getters are sometimes called property handlers.

　The syntax is as follows:

property identifier
{
	setter(argument)
	{
		// Setter content here
	}

	getter()
	{
		// Getter content here
		return expression; // Value returned as the getter
	}
}

　The setter and getter can also be written in the reverse order. 
　This property is registered as a member of the class in which this property is written, or as a global member, but getting a value from this identifier is done via the getter, and setting a value is done via the setter. These setters and getters can contain various contents just like methods. The setter takes a variable that receives the value as an argument inside the ( ) following setter. The getter returns a value using the return statement.

　The () after getter can be omitted. 

　If you write both a setter and a getter for a single identifier, it becomes a property that allows both reading and writing. If you write only one, for example if you write only a getter, it becomes a read-only property, and writing to it will result in an error.

	var value;

	property property1 // Declare property 'property1'
	{
		setter(v)
		{
			// Setter takes only one argument
			value=v; // Process argument v

			inform("value set.");
		}

		getter
		{
			// Declare getter for identifier 'property1', same as propset
			// Getter has no arguments
			inform("value get.");
			return value; // Return as return value
		}
	}

	property1=5; // Assignment to property1 calls the setter
	property1++; // In an expression like this, the getter is called,
	             // and then the setter is called to set the value
}

　Like variables, functions, and classes, properties can also be overridden.

Property Objects
　A property itself is also an object. However, when it is registered to some object, normal access methods only call the property handler, and the property object itself cannot be accessed.
　To retrieve the property object itself, use the & operator. The retrieved property object can be placed in a local variable.
　You can also use the & operator to register a property object.

	property prop // Declare property prop
	{
		(omitted)
	}

{
	var p = &prop; // Get property object and put it in a local variable

	&object.property1 = p; // Register p as property1 of object
}


　Using the & operator allows you to access the property object itself without calling the getter or setter. If you do not use the & operator, it results in normal property access.

　A property object retrieved as a local variable can have its property handler called using the * operator, even without registering it to an object.


	property prop // Declare property prop
	{
		(omitted)
	}

{
	var p = &prop; // Get property object and put it in a local variable

	*p = 30; // Call setter and set 30
	func(*p); // Call getter to retrieve value and pass to func
}


When storing a property object in a var variable as shown above, please make the variable a local variable. This is because once a property object is registered to an object, it operates via setters and getters as a property rather than as a property object. In other words, if you register a property object as a global variable (= member of global) or a member of an object instead of a local variable, it will behave like a normal property. Of course, it is fine to use the & operator to retrieve a property object registered to a global variable or object member in this way.


When using the instanceof operator on a property object with "Property", it evaluates to true (in the example above, &prop instanceof "Property" is true).
